module gunbc.fleet.cpu_freq_cap_boot_state

// A CPU FREQUENCY CEILING HELD ON A HOST BY A MECHANISM SOMEONE INSTALLED: the ceiling, the unit and script
// that hold it, what that pair read back when it finished, and -- the part that carries weight -- whether the
// ceiling still stands, and what stands in its place if it does not.
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS BEFORE ANY ACTUATOR. The fleet already has a CPU frequency ceiling on two hosts and no
// type that could say so: gunbc.plans.host_thermal_operating_point lists `ReadCpuOperatingLimits` ("governor
// and per-core limits, any power cap") as a read it PLANS to model, and nothing until now held the fact a read
// would fill. So this module declares the shape first and the rows second, and the rows that exist say what was
// read on 2026-11-24 and nothing more. No actuator, unit template or converge lives here: a ceiling the operator
// applied by hand is recorded, not reproduced. Adding the mechanism would mean a unit file, sudoers grants and a
// fleet-converge mode, and each gets modelled when there is a decision to act on, not before.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT OWN.
//   - the part's own numbers -- core count, nominal sustained per-thread clock, TDP -- are the catalog row in
//     extdeps.cpu.ampere, joined here rather than restated; a ceiling is only meaningful against them, which is
//     why `ceiling_sits_below_the_parts_nominal_clock` asks the catalog rather than a typed-in number;
//   - the watts a host draws is gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_power's, and the readings taken while this cap went in
//     are named there as confounded (load moved between them), so none of them priced anything;
//   - the decision to apply a ceiling, or to raise one, is the operator's and is recorded in
//     gunbc.design_ruling; this module reports the standing a ruling leaves behind, it cannot create one;
//   - the boot mechanism's file contents and whole content hashes are unread; each digest carries, on itself,
//     the read that would discharge it (`CapUnitDigestMarkedOnly.what_would_discharge_it` in
//     gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state), rather than a second roster saying the same thing.
//
// THE STANDING IS THE LOAD-BEARING FIELD. A ceiling standing on a host today, a ceiling the model wants on that
// host, a ceiling that was applied and has since been CHANGED, and a ceiling that was applied and whose standing
// nobody can currently answer are four facts, and the two hosts recorded here sit in the fourth: the operator
// applied 2.0 GHz on 2026-11-24, and what became of it that day is reported twice over in opposite directions --
// a relay that they were raising it, after heal's build lane ran out at its limit with the cores held there, and
// their own words the same day, "we slowed the cores on all computers". So `ceiling` names the figure that was
// APPLIED, `standing` refuses to say whether it holds, and the figure the hosts are under now is unread until a
// host read or an operator statement lands. A consumer that pushes desired state at a host must not push the
// applied figure or invent the current one, and no such consumer exists yet: there is no CPU-scaling convergence
// leg in the fleet, and writing one before the standing is readable would push a clock nobody can name. That leg
// -- and the read that must precede it -- is named as an unstarted row in
// gunbc.plans.fleet_hardware_fact_consumption's own roster, which is where this module's frontier is classified
// rather than left implied. What IS read today is consumed: gunbc.fleet.fleet_work_energy_cost
// host_draw_power_state_attribution asks `cpu_freq_cap_mechanisms_on` and
// `cpu_freq_cap_standing_discharge_read` of a host before it lets a power figure price that host's work, so the
// unsaid standing has a consequence -- the marginal cost of work on srv3 and srv4 carries the gap and the one
// read that would close it.

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr }
import std.optional { Absent, Present }
import product.host_identity { HostIdentity, host_identity_eq }
import std.measure { Hertz, hertz, hertz_count }
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity { operator_host_srv3, operator_host_srv4 }
// The cap-unit digest standing is the same question gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state first modelled for the
// GPU cap units, so it is spelled once there and imported here -- the type and the discharge read -- rather than
// minted a second time beside it.
import gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state {
  CapUnitContentDigest, CapUnitDigestMarkedOnly, declared_path_host_hash_read,
}

// What the mechanism's own script reported when it finished. The script writes the ceiling to every core and
// reads each one back, and it FAILS if any core misses -- so the count it reports is the mechanism grading its
// own work, and it is a different reading from an outside observer ticking cores over ssh.
type CpuCoreFrequencyReadBack
  = EveryCoreSeenAtCeiling { cores_read_back: Int }
  | SomeCoresMissedAtCeiling { cores_read_back: Int, cores_expected: Int }
  | ReadBackNotReported { why: NonEmptyStr }

// WHETHER THE CEILING STILL STANDS. The arms are not a workflow status and not a to-do: they are what the host
// is under. `CeilingStands` is a ceiling read holding today; `CeilingSuperseded` is a ceiling that was applied
// and has since been CHANGED -- a claim, so it carries the change's authorship and date on it; and
// `CeilingStandingUnsaid` is the third thing, which is what the two recorded hosts are: the applied figure is
// known, and whether anything replaced it is NOT known, because the reports about it conflict and no read has
// landed. It is not a hedge standing in for one of the other two: a row in this arm refuses both directions,
// answering no figure and pushing no ceiling, and it carries the competing reports rather than picking one.
// Collapsing it into `CeilingSuperseded` would write an operator act that no read has shown, and collapsing it
// into `CeilingStands` would keep pushing a clock the operator may have changed.
type CpuFreqCapStanding
  = CeilingStands { applied_on: NonEmptyStr }
  | CeilingSuperseded {
      applied_on: NonEmptyStr
      superseded_on: NonEmptyStr
      // Whose act replaced it, in their terms rather than this module's inference.
      superseded_by: NonEmptyStr
      figure_standing_today: CeilingFigureStanding
      // Why it went. Held on the row because a ceiling lifted for a build lane and a ceiling lifted for power
      // are different history, and the second reads as a policy about the hardware otherwise.
      why: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | CeilingStandingUnsaid {
      applied_on: NonEmptyStr
      // Why this model declines to say. Not "we forgot to look": what was reported, and why it does not settle
      // the question.
      unsaid_because: NonEmptyStr
      // The reports, each in its own words, kept beside each other rather than merged. A row in this arm with
      // one report is a row that has already chosen a side.
      competing_reports: List<NonEmptyStr>
      figure_standing_today: CeilingFigureStanding
    }

// WHAT FIGURE THE HOST IS UNDER NOW, as it stands for this model. Named for the question it answers rather than
// for an act: `CeilingSuperseded` already states the act, and an arm that presupposed a replacement would say
// too much for `CeilingStandingUnsaid` to carry. `FigureUnread` is the frontier: the row says what would close
// it and supplies no digits. `FigureStated` is what a host read or an operator statement lands as -- and once
// one exists, it, not the applied figure, is what the host is under.
type CeilingFigureStanding
  = FigureStated { ceiling: Hertz }
  | FigureUnread { what_would_discharge_it: NonEmptyStr }

// A frequency ceiling held on a host by an installed mechanism. The unit runs the script; both paths are the
// operator's, and both digests are as-reported markings until a host hash read lands.
type CpuFreqCapMechanism {
  host: HostIdentity
  unit_path: NonEmptyStr
  unit_digest: CapUnitContentDigest
  script_path: NonEmptyStr
  script_digest: CapUnitContentDigest
  // The ceiling the mechanism writes to each core. cpufreq's scaling_max_freq counts kilohertz; this carries
  // hertz, the unit the catalog's nominal clock uses, so the two compare without a scale silently changing.
  // This is the figure that was APPLIED -- what stands today is `standing`'s to answer.
  ceiling: Hertz
  read_back: CpuCoreFrequencyReadBack
  // Reboot-persistence as the operator stated it -- an enabled boot unit. NOT a `systemctl is-enabled` read;
  // nothing here reports what systemd says about the unit.
  persists_across_boot: Bool
  standing: CpuFreqCapStanding
  read_on: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE READ THAT WOULD TELL THIS MODEL WHAT THE HOSTS ARE UNDER NOW. Spelled once here and carried by both rows,
// because the two rows are blocked on the same missing read: no observe mode this repository models reads a CPU
// frequency limit on a host, and the only figure ever reported -- 128 of 128 cores at 2000000 kHz -- came from
// the mechanism's own script read by the operator by hand. It is the same read that would settle either
// direction, which is why the two questions -- was it raised, or does it stand -- share one discharge.
data cpu_freq_ceiling_standing_read: NonEmptyStr = "a read of what ceiling each host is under now -- either a fleet-converge observe leg that reads the CPU scaling limit on the subject host (no such mode exists today, and the first read of this mechanism was the operator's hand read rather than a modeled one), or the operator stating the figure. Until one of those lands the figure standing today is unread, and writing a number here would be inventing a host's clock in whichever direction it was written." as NonEmptyStr

// THE TWO REPORTS ABOUT WHAT HAPPENED TO THAT CEILING, kept beside each other and not merged. Both are relays
// of the operator on the same day, 2026-11-24; they do not agree, and no read of a host has landed since either.
// Held once at module level because both hosts carry exactly the same pair of reports -- a per-host copy would
// be a second place to keep the same two sentences, and the day one of them is corrected the other would stay.
data cpu_freq_cap_standing_reports: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "2026-11-24, relayed by manager session clever-raven-401: the operator was raising the srv3/srv4 CPU frequency cap, after heal's build lane timed out at its 60-minute limit six times with the cores held at 2.0 GHz and went green at 42m once the ceiling was raised. Offered as CI evidence for a motive, never as a read of a host.",
  "2026-11-24, the operator's own words through that same relay: \"we slowed the cores on all computers\" -- a cap spoken of as in force, and reaching further than srv3 and srv4.",
]

// THE TWO HOSTS THE OPERATOR HELD AT 2.0 GHZ ON 2026-11-24, and what figure stands under them now is UNSAID.
// The operator installed one unit and one script on each host that day; the applied ceiling is half the
// M128-30's nominal sustained per-thread clock, and the script's own read-back reported every core of 128 at
// it. What happened to it the same day is reported two ways that do not agree -- a relay that the operator was
// raising the srv3/srv4 ceiling, and their own words on the same day, "we slowed the cores on all computers",
// which is a cap still in force and wider than these two hosts -- and no read of a host has landed here since.
// So both rows carry `CeilingStandingUnsaid`: the applied figure and its read-back stay on the record at the
// standing the operator's own act supports, no replacement figure is written, and neither "lifted" nor "kept" is
// asserted. Both rows carry the same digest markings because the operator quoted one head-and-tail per file for
// both hosts -- whether the two hosts' copies are byte-identical is itself unread, and a hash read run on each
// host separately is what would settle that too. The reported reach, "all computers", is also wider than this
// module: no rows are written for any other host, because nothing has been read there either.
data srv3_cpu_freq_cap: CpuFreqCapMechanism = CpuFreqCapMechanism {
  host: operator_host_srv3,
  unit_path: "/etc/systemd/system/cpu-freq-cap.service" as NonEmptyStr,
  unit_digest: CapUnitDigestMarkedOnly {
    head: "35b24e44" as NonEmptyStr,
    tail: "08630" as NonEmptyStr,
    what_would_discharge_it: declared_path_host_hash_read,
  },
  script_path: "/usr/local/sbin/cpu-freq-cap.sh" as NonEmptyStr,
  script_digest: CapUnitDigestMarkedOnly {
    head: "4a940fd5" as NonEmptyStr,
    tail: "3f027" as NonEmptyStr,
    what_would_discharge_it: declared_path_host_hash_read,
  },
  ceiling: hertz(count: 2000000000),
  read_back: EveryCoreSeenAtCeiling { cores_read_back: 128 },
  persists_across_boot: true,
  standing: CeilingStandingUnsaid {
    applied_on: "2026-11-24" as NonEmptyStr,
    unsaid_because: "what was APPLIED is the operator's own act, reported by them together with the script's own read-back. What happened to it afterwards is reported twice, in two directions, and nothing has been read on either host since: a row here would be one of those reports chosen over the other. Both are carried on this row and neither is ruled on -- gunbc.design_ruling::operator_cpu_freq_cap_standing_unsaid_2026_11_24." as NonEmptyStr,
    competing_reports: cpu_freq_cap_standing_reports,
    figure_standing_today: FigureUnread {
      what_would_discharge_it: cpu_freq_ceiling_standing_read,
    },
  },
  read_on: "2026-11-24, applied and read by the operator by hand over the admin route; the operator's words are gunbc.design_ruling::operator_hand_applied_srv3_srv4_power_caps_2026_11_24" as NonEmptyStr,
}

data srv4_cpu_freq_cap: CpuFreqCapMechanism = CpuFreqCapMechanism {
  host: operator_host_srv4,
  unit_path: "/etc/systemd/system/cpu-freq-cap.service" as NonEmptyStr,
  unit_digest: CapUnitDigestMarkedOnly {
    head: "35b24e44" as NonEmptyStr,
    tail: "08630" as NonEmptyStr,
    what_would_discharge_it: declared_path_host_hash_read,
  },
  script_path: "/usr/local/sbin/cpu-freq-cap.sh" as NonEmptyStr,
  script_digest: CapUnitDigestMarkedOnly {
    head: "4a940fd5" as NonEmptyStr,
    tail: "3f027" as NonEmptyStr,
    what_would_discharge_it: declared_path_host_hash_read,
  },
  ceiling: hertz(count: 2000000000),
  read_back: EveryCoreSeenAtCeiling { cores_read_back: 128 },
  persists_across_boot: true,
  standing: CeilingStandingUnsaid {
    applied_on: "2026-11-24" as NonEmptyStr,
    unsaid_because: "what was APPLIED is the operator's own act, reported by them together with the script's own read-back. What happened to it afterwards is reported twice, in two directions, and nothing has been read on either host since: a row here would be one of those reports chosen over the other. Both are carried on this row and neither is ruled on -- gunbc.design_ruling::operator_cpu_freq_cap_standing_unsaid_2026_11_24." as NonEmptyStr,
    competing_reports: cpu_freq_cap_standing_reports,
    figure_standing_today: FigureUnread {
      what_would_discharge_it: cpu_freq_ceiling_standing_read,
    },
  },
  read_on: "2026-11-24, applied and read by the operator by hand over the admin route; the operator's words are gunbc.design_ruling::operator_hand_applied_srv3_srv4_power_caps_2026_11_24" as NonEmptyStr,
}

data cpu_freq_cap_mechanisms: List<CpuFreqCapMechanism> = [ srv3_cpu_freq_cap, srv4_cpu_freq_cap ]

// WHEN THE FIGURE WAS PUT ON THE CORES. Held inside the standing -- every arm carries its own date -- so the
// answer comes off the value the row already has rather than a fourth field restating it. A consumer that names
// the gap a ceiling leaves needs the date it was applied to say when the figure stopped being evidence.
fn cpu_freq_cap_applied_on(m: CpuFreqCapMechanism) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match m.standing {
    CeilingStands { applied_on } => applied_on
    CeilingSuperseded { applied_on, superseded_on: _, superseded_by: _, figure_standing_today: _, why: _ } => applied_on
    CeilingStandingUnsaid { applied_on, unsaid_because: _, competing_reports: _, figure_standing_today: _ } => applied_on
  }
}

fn cpu_freq_cap_mechanisms_on(host: HostIdentity) -> List<CpuFreqCapMechanism> {
  filter(cpu_freq_cap_mechanisms, m => host_identity_eq(a: m.host, b: host))
}

// THE OPEN READ A CEILING'S RECORD CARRIES: what would tell this model what the host is under now. Absent when
// the ceiling was read holding today, or when a figure has already landed. This is the frontier, and it lives in
// the standing rather than beside it, so a figure cannot be stated in one place while another row still calls
// the ceiling unread -- and the same read answers whichever way it turns out, which is why one discharge serves
// both the superseded and the unsaid arm.
fn cpu_freq_cap_standing_discharge_read(m: CpuFreqCapMechanism) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match m.standing {
    CeilingStands { applied_on: _ } => Absent
    CeilingSuperseded {
      applied_on: _, superseded_on: _, superseded_by: _, figure_standing_today: figure, why: _,
    } =>
      match figure {
        FigureUnread { what_would_discharge_it } => Present { value: what_would_discharge_it }
        FigureStated { ceiling: _ } => Absent
      }
    CeilingStandingUnsaid {
      applied_on: _, unsaid_because: _, competing_reports: _, figure_standing_today: figure,
    } =>
      match figure {
        FigureUnread { what_would_discharge_it } => Present { value: what_would_discharge_it }
        FigureStated { ceiling: _ } => Absent
      }
  }
}

// Is the APPLIED figure below the clock the installed part is rated to sustain? Asked of the catalog row rather
// than of a number typed into a row, so a ceiling that quietly rose to the part's nominal clock would stop being
// called a cap. This compares what was applied on 2026-11-24; it says nothing about what stands today, which is
// `cpu_freq_ceiling_standing_on`'s to answer.
fn ceiling_sits_below_the_parts_nominal_clock(m: CpuFreqCapMechanism, nominal: Hertz) -> Bool {
  hertz_count(h: m.ceiling) < hertz_count(h: nominal)
}
